---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PromptBlock from '../../components/PromptBlock.astro';

const recipes = [
  {
    id: 'discovery',
    label: 'Discovery to first PR',
    title: 'From idea to first PR, with the thinking captured',
    outcome: 'Half a day of unstructured exploration becomes a tracked epic with one PR in the queue, ready to review.',
    summary: "When you're starting from something fuzzier than a spec. You'll prototype to learn, grill yourself on what you learned, write the PRD that captures the decision, split it into issues, and TDD the first one.",
    chain: [
      { skill: 'prototype', label: 'Prototype', body: 'Build a throwaway version to feel the shape of the design. State, edges, UI variants, whichever is fuzzy.' },
      { skill: 'grill-me', label: 'Grill yourself', body: "Force the questions you've been avoiding. Failure modes, partial state, concurrency, scale, telemetry, rollback. Every branch gets an answer." },
      { skill: 'to-prd', label: 'Write the PRD', body: 'Turn the prototype and the grilling answers into a PRD: problem, constraints, decisions, non-goals. Saved to your project tracker.' },
      { skill: 'to-issues', label: 'Split into issues', body: 'Break the PRD into tracer-bullet vertical slices, one per issue. Each issue is sized to land as one PR.' },
      { skill: 'tdd', label: 'TDD the first slice', body: 'Red-green-refactor on the first issue. Commits at every transition so the loop is visible in the diff.' },
    ],
    kickoffLabel: 'Discovery kickoff',
    kickoffPrompt: `I want to build the bulk-CSV import feature. We need to handle 50k rows, validate against the existing user schema, and produce a downloadable error report.

Let's go through the discovery chain:
1. Prototype both the streaming-parse approach and the chunked-batch approach so I can feel the difference.
2. Grill me on the design: failure modes, partial uploads, concurrent users, what error reporting actually looks like.
3. Write the PRD from what we landed on, save it to Linear.
4. Split into issues sized for one PR each.
5. TDD the first issue.

Stop after each step so I can review.`,
    when: "Use when: you have an ask, not a spec. You don't yet know the right design.",
  },
  {
    id: 'shipping',
    label: 'Stacked-PR shipping',
    title: 'Stacked-PR shipping, end to end',
    outcome: 'A planned feature lands as a chain of small reviewable PRs, each rebased cleanly after the previous one merges.',
    summary: "When the spec already exists. You'll plan it as an epic, build it as a stack of PRs, address review comments, rebase the next one when the previous merges, and reset cleanly between tasks.",
    chain: [
      { skill: 'spec', label: 'Spec the feature', body: 'Refine the ask into a parent epic with ordered sub-issues. Each sub-issue is scoped to a single PR. No code yet.' },
      { skill: 'implement', label: 'Implement the stack', body: 'Build the epic as stacked branches, one PR per sub-issue. Stops at PR boundaries so you can review before continuing.' },
      { skill: 'review', label: 'Address reviews', body: 'Walk PR comments grouped by file. Each one gets a fix-and-reply, a decline-with-rationale, or an ask-for-clarification.' },
      { skill: 'topr', label: 'Rebase the next PR', body: 'After PR n merges via squash, rebase PR n+1 cleanly. The skill correctly drops the squash-merged commits.' },
      { skill: 'next', label: 'Reset', body: "Clean throwaway branch on the latest main. The next task starts from a known good state; the old branch stays in case you need it." },
    ],
    kickoffLabel: 'Shipping kickoff',
    kickoffPrompt: `Spec the bulk-CSV import feature into a tracked epic with ordered sub-issues. Each sub-issue should be scoped to a single PR.

Once the spec is approved, implement it as a stack of PRs. Stop after PR #2 so I can review.

After each PR merges, run /impeccable audit on the changed frontend files to catch design regressions before addressing review comments. Then rebase the next PR on main and reset for the following task.`,
    when: 'Use when: the design decisions are made. You know what to build and just need it shipped methodically.',
  },
  {
    id: 'frontend',
    label: 'Frontend feature, design-aware',
    title: 'Frontend feature, design-aware',
    outcome: 'A UI feature ships with engineering discipline AND design craft, not one at the expense of the other.',
    summary: "When the work is user-facing. Pace owns the engineering loop; impeccable owns the visual + interaction craft. They pair through a Cowork session where you route between them.",
    chain: [
      { skill: '/impeccable shape', label: 'Shape the UX', body: 'Plan the screens, states, copy, and edge cases before writing real code. Impeccable thinks in design; Pace will think in tests next.' },
      { skill: 'prototype', label: 'Prototype', body: 'Build a runnable throwaway with several UI variants on one route. Toggle between them to feel which design holds up.' },
      { skill: 'tdd', label: 'TDD the logic underneath', body: 'Write the failing tests for the behavior the design implies. Keep the prototype open so the tests reflect what you actually want.' },
      { skill: 'implement', label: 'Implement', body: 'Promote the prototype + tests into real code. The tests stay green throughout; the design choices land as code.' },
      { skill: '/impeccable polish', label: 'Polish', body: 'Typography, color, motion, accessibility, and spacing pass on the implemented feature. Catches what TDD cannot see.' },
      { skill: 'ship-pr', label: 'Ship the PR', body: "Commit, push, open the PR. The PR description includes both the engineering test plan and the design rationale (impeccable's notes from the polish pass)." },
    ],
    kickoffLabel: 'Frontend feature kickoff',
    kickoffPrompt: `We're building the new account-settings page. Routes between impeccable (for design) and the engineering plugin (for code) across the session:

1. /impeccable shape the page: which states exist (loading, empty, error, multi-account), what the copy says, what the keyboard flow is.
2. Prototype two layouts so I can pick: tabs on the left vs. cards in a grid.
3. TDD the underlying logic (account switching, edit-then-save, optimistic UI rollback on error).
4. Implement against the tests.
5. /impeccable polish: typography hierarchy, color, motion on the save action, a11y on the form.
6. Ship the PR with both the engineering test plan and the design rationale.

Stop after step 2 so I can pick the layout.`,
    when: "Use when: the work is user-facing. Don't skip the polish pass; that's where the design actually lands.",
  },
];

interface SkillEntry {
  name: string;
  triggers: string[];
  example: string;
  produces: string;
}

// Skill reference kept for potential future use; not rendered on this page.
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars
const _skills: Record<string, SkillEntry> = {
  spec: {
    name: 'Refine a feature into a tracked epic. Each sub-issue maps to one PR.',
    triggers: ['"Spec the new export feature."', '"Plan this out before we build it."', '"Break this initiative into issues."'],
    example: '"Spec the bulk-CSV import feature. Should handle 50k rows, validate against the existing user schema, and produce a downloadable error report."',
    produces: 'A parent epic issue + ordered child issues, each scoped to a single PR. No code yet; hand off to /implement when ready.',
  },
  implement: {
    name: 'Execute a /spec-produced epic as a chain of stacked PRs, one per sub-issue.',
    triggers: ['"Implement epic #142."', '"Build out the spec we wrote."', '"Start on issue #143."'],
    example: '"Implement epic #142. Stop after PR #2 so I can review."',
    produces: 'A branch per sub-issue stacked on the previous one, each with a focused PR. Stops cleanly so you can review before continuing.',
  },
  review: {
    name: 'Address PR review comments interactively. Fix or decline with reasoning, post inline replies.',
    triggers: ['"Address the PR comments on #156."', '"Go through the review queue."', '"Clear the comments on this PR."'],
    example: '"Walk me through the review on PR #156. Fix the obvious ones, push back on the ones you disagree with."',
    produces: 'Comments grouped by file, each triaged with an action: fix-and-reply, decline-with-rationale, or ask-for-clarification. Inline replies posted.',
  },
  topr: {
    name: 'Rebase a stacked PR onto the latest origin/main after an earlier PR in the stack merged.',
    triggers: ['"Rebase PR #157 on main."', '"Refresh this PR after #156 merged."', '"Top of stack, rebase."'],
    example: '"PR #156 just merged via squash-and-merge. Rebase #157 on main."',
    produces: 'A clean rebase that correctly drops the commits that came in via the squash-merge of the earlier PR. No duplicate commits, no manual conflict-resolution where the rebase tool gets it wrong.',
  },
  next: {
    name: 'Reset between tasks. Checks out a clean throwaway branch on the latest main so the next task starts fresh.',
    triggers: ['"Next."', '"Reset for the next thing."', '"Done with this, what\'s next?"'],
    example: '"Next."',
    produces: 'Switch to a clean dummy branch rebased on latest main. Old branch left intact in case you need to come back.',
  },
  'grill-me': {
    name: 'Interview yourself relentlessly about a plan or design until every decision branch has an answer.',
    triggers: ['"Grill me on this design."', '"Stress-test the plan."', '"Poke holes in this."'],
    example: '"Grill me on the bulk-CSV import design. I think we should stream parse, you tell me what I\'m missing."',
    produces: 'A back-and-forth Q+A where Claude pushes on the design: what about partial failures, what about huge files, what about concurrent uploads, what does the user see on error. Stops when every branch has a concrete answer.',
  },
  'grill-with-docs': {
    name: 'Same as grill-me, but checks your plan against the existing CONTEXT.md, ADRs, and inline domain language.',
    triggers: ['"Grill this against our docs."', '"Pressure-test using project context."'],
    example: '"Grill me on the bulk-CSV import. Make sure I\'m using the same vocabulary we use in CONTEXT.md."',
    produces: 'A grilling session that catches inconsistencies between your plan and what the project already documents. Updates docs inline as decisions firm up.',
  },
  'zoom-out': {
    name: 'Ask Claude to step back and give the higher-level shape of the code you\'re looking at.',
    triggers: ['"Zoom out, what is this doing at a system level?"', '"Give me the architecture view of this module."'],
    example: '"I\'m in the payment-retry code and I don\'t see the larger flow. Zoom out."',
    produces: 'A description of how the current code fits the larger system: who calls it, what state it depends on, what comes after, what failure modes exist at the boundary.',
  },
  'careful-review': {
    name: 'Re-read what you just wrote with fresh eyes and look hard for bugs, dead code, or confusing patterns. Then fix them.',
    triggers: ['"Careful review."', '"Look it over again."', '"Give it another pass before I commit."'],
    example: '"I just rewrote the auth middleware. Careful review before I push."',
    produces: 'A pass through the diff with concrete findings: edge case not handled, variable name that misleads, error that swallows instead of surfacing, test that doesn\'t actually assert anything. Fixes applied.',
  },
  'codex-review': {
    name: 'Run an OpenAI Codex code review for a structured second opinion. Confirms P0/P1 findings against the code, fixes confirmed ones, commits.',
    triggers: ['"Codex review."', '"Get a second opinion."', '"Run Codex on this branch."'],
    example: '"Run Codex review against main on this branch before I open the PR."',
    produces: 'A summary of Codex\'s findings, separated into "real bug, fixed" vs. "false positive, ignored" with the rationale per finding. Confirmed fixes committed.',
  },
  tdd: {
    name: 'Test-driven development with red-green-refactor. Write the failing test first, then minimal code to make it pass, then refactor.',
    triggers: ['"TDD this feature."', '"Red-green-refactor."', '"Test-first."'],
    example: '"TDD the discount-rule engine. Write the failing tests for the percentage discount first, then make them pass."',
    produces: 'A test file with red tests, then a minimal implementation that makes them green, then refactoring with the tests staying green throughout. Commits at each transition so you can see the loop.',
  },
  'find-missing-tests': {
    name: 'Survey a module, file, or PR for missing coverage. Produces a specific test backlog. Optionally opens GitHub issues for each gap.',
    triggers: ['"Find missing tests."', '"What\'s not tested in this module?"', '"Test backlog for src/billing."'],
    example: '"Find missing tests in src/billing. Open issues for anything that should exist but doesn\'t."',
    produces: 'A list of specific test cases that should exist, each detailed enough that another agent (or developer) can implement it. Not "test the validator" but "test that validator rejects negative amounts with a specific error code."',
  },
  'e2e-test': {
    name: 'Run a full end-to-end test against real backend + frontend dev servers, with mocks disabled. Verifies a release before shipping.',
    triggers: ['"E2E sweep."', '"Smoke test the app."', '"Test everything end to end before I deploy."'],
    example: '"E2E sweep. Start the dev servers, drive the full signup → first-purchase flow, give me a PASS/FAIL summary."',
    produces: 'A PASS or FAIL summary of the critical user flows, with logs and screenshots of any failure. No mocks, no stubs, real network.',
  },
  diagnose: {
    name: 'Disciplined diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Reproduce, minimize, hypothesize, instrument, fix, write the regression test.',
    triggers: ['"Diagnose this bug."', '"Debug this perf regression."', '"Something is broken in prod, walk through it methodically."'],
    example: '"The /checkout endpoint is 10x slower than yesterday\'s build. Diagnose."',
    produces: 'A reproducible minimal case, a hypothesis with evidence, the actual fix, and a regression test that would have caught it. The methodical version of a bug hunt.',
  },
  debug: {
    name: 'Structured debugging session for a specific error message, stack trace, or "works locally, broken in prod" situation.',
    triggers: ['"Debug this stack trace."', '"This works in staging but not prod."', '"Why is this throwing?"'],
    example: '"Getting `TypeError: cannot read properties of undefined (reading \'tokenize\')` in the lexer. Debug."',
    produces: 'A walkthrough: where the value should have been set, why it wasn\'t, what the fix is, what test would have caught it.',
  },
  'security-review': {
    name: 'Security-focused review covering OWASP top-10 + language-specific footguns. Prioritized findings with concrete fixes.',
    triggers: ['"Security review."', '"Is this safe to expose?"', '"Check for vulnerabilities before we ship."'],
    example: '"Security review the new file-upload endpoint. It accepts user-controlled filenames."',
    produces: 'A prioritized list of findings: P0 (must fix), P1 (should fix), P2 (worth noting), each with a concrete patch. Covers injection, auth bypass, IDOR, SSRF, secrets in code, unsafe deserialization, plus language-specific traps.',
  },
  prototype: {
    name: 'Build a throwaway prototype to learn from before committing to a design. Runnable terminal app for logic questions, or several UI variants on one route for design questions.',
    triggers: ['"Prototype this."', '"Let me play with the data model."', '"Try a few designs."'],
    example: '"Prototype three different ways the empty state for the dashboard could look. Toggle between them with a query param."',
    produces: 'A working throwaway you can run and feel: not pretty code, but something you can play with for an hour to learn whether the underlying design is right.',
  },
  'ship-pr': {
    name: 'Commit all changes safely, push the branch, and open a pull request with a summary and test plan.',
    triggers: ['"Ship it."', '"Open the PR."', '"Ship the PR."', '"Done, push it up."'],
    example: '"Ship the PR. Title: feat: bulk-CSV import (rule engine)."',
    produces: 'Staged commit (skipping .env / credentials / secrets), commit message matching the repo\'s style, pre-commit hooks passing, PR opened against the default branch with a description and a test-plan checklist.',
  },
};
---

<Base
  title="Engineering cookbook · Pace"
  description="Deep-dive engineering recipes: the stacked-PR workflow (spec → implement → review → topr → next), TDD, careful-review, diagnose, security-review, ship-pr, grill-me, and the rest of the engineering plugin's 34 skills."
  activeNav="cookbook"
  canonicalPath="/cookbook/engineering"
>
  <article class="usecases-page">
    <header class="usecases-header">
      <span class="usecases-eyebrow">Engineering cookbook</span>
      <p class="usecases-back-link"><a href="/cookbook">&larr; Back to all cookbooks</a></p>
      <h1>Engineering cookbook</h1>
      <p class="usecases-lede">
        Three recipes (skill chains) cover most engineering work. Recipes are how skills compose into a finished result: one for fuzzy discovery, one for methodical shipping, and one for user-facing work that pairs Pace's engineering loop with <a href="https://impeccable.style">impeccable</a>'s design craft.
      </p>
      <div class="eng-install-row">
        <a class="usecase-step-btn" href="https://claude.ai/desktop/customize/plugins/new?marketplace=GoldenBerry-SO/Pace&plugin=engineering" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Install engineering in Cowork &nearr;</a>
        <code class="usecase-step-cmd">claude plugin install engineering@pace</code>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="eng-toc" aria-label="Jump to recipe">
      <span class="eng-toc-label">Jump to a recipe</span>
      <ul>
        {recipes.map((r) => (
          <li><a href={`#recipe-${r.id}`}>{r.label}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section class="eng-recipes" id="recipes">
      <header class="eng-section-head">
        <h2>Recipes</h2>
        <p>Skills compose. A real engineering task usually runs three to five skills in order, with the output of one feeding the next. Two recipe shapes cover most work.</p>
      </header>

      {recipes.map((r) => (
        <article class="eng-recipe" id={`recipe-${r.id}`}>
          <header class="eng-recipe-head">
            <span class="eng-recipe-badge">Recipe</span>
            <h3>{r.title}</h3>
            <p class="eng-recipe-outcome">{r.outcome}</p>
            <p class="eng-recipe-summary">{r.summary}</p>
          </header>

          <ol class="eng-recipe-chain">
            {r.chain.map((step, i) => (
              <li class="eng-recipe-step">
                <div class="eng-recipe-step-num">{i + 1}</div>
                <div class="eng-recipe-step-body">
                  <div class="eng-recipe-step-head">
                    <code class="eng-recipe-step-skill">{step.skill}</code>
                    <h4>{step.label}</h4>
                  </div>
                  <p set:html={step.body}></p>
                </div>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>

          <div class="eng-recipe-kickoff">
            <PromptBlock prompt={r.kickoffPrompt} label={r.kickoffLabel} />
          </div>

          <p class="eng-recipe-when">{r.when}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="eng-foot">
      <h3>Want every skill spelled out?</h3>
      <p>
        The <a href="/docs/skills">skill catalog</a> lists every one of the 34 engineering skills with the SKILL.md frontmatter, trigger phrases, and source files. Use it as a reference; come back here for the recipes that compose them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not on engineering? The <a href="/cookbook">main cookbook</a> has the per-team marquee recipes.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .usecases-page {
    max-width: 920px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--spacing-xl) var(--spacing-lg) var(--spacing-3xl);
  }
  .usecases-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-2xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-lg);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-mist);
  }
  .usecases-back-link {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    color: var(--color-ash);
    margin: var(--spacing-sm) 0;
  }
  .usecases-back-link a {
    color: var(--color-ash);
  }
  .usecases-back-link a:hover {
    color: var(--color-forest);
  }
  .usecases-header h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-3xl);
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    margin: var(--spacing-md) 0 var(--spacing-md);
  }
  .usecases-lede {
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-lg);
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .eng-install-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .usecase-step-cmd {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    background: var(--color-code-bg);
    padding: 8px 14px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-code-border);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    user-select: all;
  }

  /* TOC */
  .eng-toc {
    margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-2xl);
    padding: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
    background: var(--color-paper-elev);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-mist);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }
  .eng-toc-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-ash);
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  .eng-toc ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px 18px;
  }
  .eng-toc a {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-mist);
    padding-bottom: 2px;
    transition: color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out-quart),
                border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }
  .eng-toc a:hover {
    color: var(--color-forest);
    border-color: var(--color-forest);
  }

  /* Recipes */
  .eng-recipes {
    margin: var(--spacing-2xl) 0;
    padding-top: var(--spacing-xl);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-mist);
  }
  .eng-recipe {
    background: var(--color-paper-elev);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-mist);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: var(--spacing-lg) var(--spacing-xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-lg);
  }
  .eng-recipe-head {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-mist);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-lg);
  }
  .eng-recipe-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-forest);
    background: transparent;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-forest-soft);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 4px 8px;
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
  }
  .eng-recipe-badge::before {
    content: "";
    display: inline-block;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    background: currentColor;
  }
  .eng-recipe-head h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-xl);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-sm);
  }
  .eng-recipe-outcome {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-lg);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--color-forest);
    margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-sm);
    padding: 12px 16px;
    background: var(--color-forest-dim);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-forest);
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  .eng-recipe-summary {
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .eng-recipe-chain {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-lg);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .eng-recipe-step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 38px 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    background: var(--color-paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-mist);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
  .eng-recipe-step-num {
    width: 30px;
    height: 30px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-forest);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .eng-recipe-step-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 10px;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .eng-recipe-step-skill {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-forest);
    background: var(--color-forest-dim);
    padding: 2px 8px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    border: 0;
  }
  .eng-recipe-step-body h4 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .eng-recipe-step-body p {
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .eng-recipe-kickoff {
    margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0 var(--spacing-md);
  }
  .eng-recipe-when {
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
